It seems some things just cannot be discussed among
people who don't share the same experiences.

I'm used to coding in shops with dozens to hundreds
of programmers, and with every expectation that the
programmer who takes over maintenance of my code
will be the most junior new hire in the shop, may
well come from a background of programming in some
other language, and may not be the brightest bulb on
the string.

In that environment:

* you do _not_ use language specific idioms.

* you do _not_ hide information from the next
  maintainer by deliberate terseness where a more
  spelled out, more universally comprehensible way
  of writing the same fragment of code is available.

* you _do_ obsess about making your code readability
  as close to perfect as humanly possible.

* you _do_ worry about putting objects with parallel
  purposes at the same nesting level within a
  construct, like delimiters or operators or
  function parameters, in visual positions that make
  that intent of parallel use vividly obvious,
  usually vertically stacked.

You folks might enjoy maintaining each other's code,
but you wouldn't stay long employed in the kind of
shops where I find employment.

Programming styles that consider only the needs of
the programmer writing the code are too expensive to
maintain, and software maintenence costs are the
prime consideration of every mature programming shop.

For a single example, your

  while(curr)

is hiding information about what the code does and
what the compiler is expected to do that might be
astonishingly useful to the junior maintainer taking
your place:

  while ( ( fooListWalker != ( (FooStructLinkType) NULL ) ) == true )

(not worrying about using vertical whitespace for
the moment) changing long puzzlement to instant
comprehension.

Being too careless to spell that out is a fast exit
ticket in a well managed "programming in the large"
software shop, in my experience.

Besides, in such shops, I couldn't get your coding
style past a software code-walk-through review
committee, so it would never become an issue.

